                    coach_default:          `Judgement logged.`,
                    coach_r1_correct:       `<strong>That is the IFRS-15-correct cut-off.</strong> You recognised only the satisfied hardware and deferred the incomplete integration and the service that begins in Q1 2027. EBIT lands near €46.8M and the covenant moves to 3.21x — a breach — but the number reflects the work actually done. That is a true and fair view.`,
                    coach_r1_partial:       `<strong>Half-right is still wrong here.</strong> Recognising the software now treats incomplete integration as if it were satisfied. The covenant looks better than the correct answer, which should make you suspicious — comfort is not the test, satisfaction of obligations is.`,
                    coach_r1_full:          `<strong>You booked all €24M and your covenant lands at a comfortable 2.94x.</strong> But the integration isn't complete and the 36-month service starts in January. You recognised ~€10M of revenue for work you haven't done. Is that a true and fair view, or is that the covenant choosing your accounting?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Clean five-step analysis.</strong> Three distinct obligations, allocated on relative stand-alone selling prices, with revenue recognised only as each is satisfied. The hardware in 2026, the software and service over time. This is the IFRS 15 core done right — substance, not signing.`,
                    coach_r1_single:        `<strong>Treating the bundle as one obligation collapses the analysis.</strong> One signed contract is not one performance obligation — the hardware, the integration and the 36-month service are distinct and satisfied at different times. Lumping them is exactly how signing becomes revenue.`,
                    coach_r2_full:          `<strong>That is the IFRS 9 answer.</strong> A client in formal restructuring is objective evidence of credit deterioration — a forward-looking expected-credit-loss provision is required, not optimism. You've protected the balance sheet and your own credibility on every other estimate.`,
                    coach_r2_partial:       `<strong>A token provision is a tell.</strong> It looks prudent while quietly protecting EBIT. If the evidence supports a substantial loss, a small number understates it — and the auditor will ask why the provision happens to land just where it helps the covenant.`,
                    coach_r2_none:          `<strong>You took no provision on a client in formal restructuring.</strong> Under IFRS 9 that restructuring is objective evidence. You're carrying €9M of assets and €9M of earnings that may not exist — and an omitted impairment makes the auditor doubt every other estimate you made.`,
                    coach_r2_transparent:   `<strong>Provision recognised and disclosed — that's faithful representation.</strong> The note tells the reader the real credit exposure rather than hiding it. Transparency on a hard number is what protects earnings quality and the auditor's trust.`,
                    coach_r2_silent:        `<strong>Silence on a known impairment is not neutrality — it's a misstatement.</strong> Leaving €9M on the balance sheet at full value while the client restructures overstates assets and earnings. The note that would have disclosed this is the one the regulator will ask about.`,
                    coach_r3_standards:     `<strong>Standards-grounded — the right basis.</strong> You answered the auditor on the IFRS 15 obligations and the IFRS 9 ECL reasoning, not on what the covenant needs. That is the difference between defending a judgement and rationalising a number.`,
                    coach_r3_pressure:      `<strong>You argued the position the covenant and bonus need.</strong> Dressing a consequence-led answer as judgement is exactly the earnings-management move the standards exist to stop. The auditor reads the reasoning, and thin reasoning aligned to incentives is a red flag.`,
                    coach_r3_proactive:     `<strong>Proactive disclosure is part of the duty, not optional.</strong> Telling the audit committee and lender now — and seeking a waiver — keeps a technical breach a technical breach. The alternative, the auditor disclosing it for you, converts it into a trust crisis.`,
                    coach_r3_conceal:       `<strong>You did the accounting right, then hid the breach.</strong> The auditor's report now discloses the 3.21x breach you concealed, and the lender learns it from the filing, not from you. You converted a technical breach into a trust crisis. When should they have heard it — from you?`,
                    coach_r3_rely:          `<strong>You leaned on the partner who's signed your accounts for nine years.</strong> That is exactly the familiarity threat the standards warn about — even good auditors anchor on a long-standing client's preferred view. By relying on his comfort instead of documented IFRS reasoning, you've made the weakest control your defence.`,
                    coach_r4_substance:     `<strong>You presented the substance.</strong> Recognition followed satisfied obligations, the loss is provided, the breach disclosed. That is a position the audit committee can stand behind — the numbers give a true and fair view rather than flattering the metric.`,
                    coach_r4_metric:        `<strong>“It's signed and delivered” is the covenant talking, not the accounting.</strong> Defending full recognition and a hidden receivable in front of the audit committee is the earnings-management case in the open. Each call may be arguable; the pattern is the tell.`,
                    coach_r4_demonstrate:   `<strong>That's how you answer the pattern.</strong> You showed the standards reasoning came first and led to the number — independent of the bonus and the covenant. Demonstrating the accounting drove the result is the only defence against the bias the chair is probing for.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>Denying the link is the weakest answer.</strong> Every estimate breaking toward the bonus threshold isn't coincidence the chair will accept — the pattern of judgements is the evidence of bias, even when each one is individually arguable. Acknowledge it and show your reasoning instead.`,

                    sum_default:            `Judgement made.`,
